  /* Unit 1 · Lovernance: an overview of three kinds of land (cards linking to #lens-governance/1-1 etc.), then 1.1 Needs-meeting commons, 1.2 Collective-owned homes, 1.3 Give-and-get program. Law rows start with a word (Helps, In the way, County by county, Federal, Not yet law), never colour alone. */
  var UNIT1 = "<div class=\"gv-unit\" id=\"gv-u1\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">Unit 1</span><h2>Lovernance</h2><p><b><i>“To change something, build a new model that makes the existing model obsolete.”</i></b> Credited to Buckminster Fuller.</p></div><section class=\"gv-sec\"><p>I’m calling my envisioned alternatives lovernance: what I want the law to allow and encourage now. As our shared thinking catches up, communities can hold it themselves and government can step back. Every situation is case-by-case. This unit starts from where you live: three generalized kinds of land, each set up through zoning, and what changes if you rent. Unit 2 shows how it all works together.</p><p>All are built on kinship; nobody charges their sister rent. What we give flows from relationship and need, and care reaches outward (especially once our needs feel met), from our household to loved ones, neighbors, and strangers, including animals, plants, and land.</p><p>All start in the same place: the minimum isn’t what rent costs, it’s what it costs to meet needs directly. Rain caught on a tarp, seeds from a seed library and cuttings from a neighbor cost close to nothing (<a href=\"#lens-water/sources\">Water: Sources</a>). The one thing that takes money is land, so that’s where governance comes in.</p><div class=\"gv-visions\"><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1.1 · Public</span><h3>Needs-meeting commons</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">The engine: shared spaces and dense homes where the most people’s needs get met.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/1-1\">Read 1.1 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1.2 · Owned together</span><h3>Collective-owned homes</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Homes held together, from dense commons homes to a single suburban house.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/1-2\">Read 1.2 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1.3 · Private</span><h3>Give-and-get program</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Homes people own that opt in to share, and everyone living in a collective-owned home.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/1-3\">Read 1.3 ↓</a></article><article class=\"gv-vision\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">1.4 · Renting</span><h3>If you rent</h3><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Renters go first at the commons, with more ways to stay and to own.</p><a href=\"#lens-governance/1-4\">Read 1.4 ↓</a></article></div><h4>Two ways in</h4><div class=\"gv-axes\"><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">Home · Step in</span><p>Give-and-get: share a garden bed, a guest spot or a foster animal, and get support back (1.3).</p></div><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">Home · All in</span><p>A collective-owned home, with standing that moves with you (1.2).</p></div><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">Work · Step in</span><p>A fair-rate contract: meet a need at a rate the town publishes (2.1).</p></div><div><span class=\"eyebrow\">Work · All in</span><p>A needs-meeting business: fair shares, elected leaders, public pay (2.1).</p></div></div><p>Step in the way you’d join a grocery co-op, and go all in when you’re ready. Home and work can overlap, like working from home. Nobody has to leave real safety to try.</p></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-1-1\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">1.1</span><div><h2>Needs-meeting commons</h2><p>Public</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">One fallback that isn’t a punishment: a good place to live where your needs are met.</p><p>Anyone can walk in, especially people with nowhere else to go. Most commons won’t be built from scratch; they’ll be conversions. The house next door becomes a commons, and the whole block gains a station for getting needs met. Want a different life? You’re free to try your luck elsewhere.</p><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Day one:</b> a bed, water and food. Rest comes first, and nobody is asked to work at the door.</li><li><b>A station for the block:</b> water refills, showers, toilets, laundry, a pantry, thrift, repair and a tool library, open to neighbors.</li><li><b>Food for residents first,</b> then outward to neighbors.</li><li><b>Play and gather:</b> sports fields, courts, pools, stages, dance and music halls, and game rooms, open to everyone.</li><li><b>A public materials store:</b> a thrift run by the commons, with lumber, fabric, tiles and salvage for anyone building or mending, and cheap hardware at the tool library.</li><li><b>Foster animals.</b> Shelter dogs, cats and rabbits can live with residents who want them, with food, vet care and backup care provided.</li><li><b>Work that meets real needs.</b> Worker co-ops are like unions that own the shop, and existing unions are friends: a plumber meets real needs too. The direction is “I’m on call in the neighborhood to fix your pipe, and my family has a home,” not price-gouging for executives.</li><li><b>Worker co-ops</b> make what people need: beds, natural-fiber clothing, tools, solar, internet and seed.</li><li><b>One price for everyone.</b> When there isn’t enough, need comes first and residents get a set discount.</li><li><b>One at a time:</b> a bed, not 75 beds to resell. Trade in an old model toward a new one, and the old one gets repaired or passed on.</li><li><b>Not a tragedy.</b> Commons last when the people who use them make the rules, with small responses first and conflicts worked through close to home (Elinor Ostrom; <a href=\"#lens-relationships/dialogue\">Dialogue</a>).</li><li><b>The land</b> is held by a community land trust. Members give time instead of money. <b>Rentals can become co-ops</b> that residents own, at real cost.</li><li><b>Anyone can leave,</b> with their skills and credit for what they gave.</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span><i>City of Santa Barbara v. Adamson</i> (1980): the state right to privacy struck down a cap on unrelated people sharing a house.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Assessors must count a land trust’s resale limits, keeping property tax lower (§402.1).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Williamson Act (1965) taxes contracted farmland as a farm. The Rainwater Capture Act (2012) lets you use roof rain without a water right.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Worker Cooperative Act (2015), plus SB 1079 (2020) and San Francisco’s COPA (2019), which give tenants, land trusts and co-ops a chance to buy when certain homes sell.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> In a declared shelter crisis, a city or county can swap state building and zoning rules for simpler local ones, but only for shelters on public land (Gov. Code §8698.4, until 2036).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">County by county</span> Class K lets people build simpler rural homes, only where adopted. Shelter and safe-parking rules vary too.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> State policy is that no adoptable animal be euthanized if it can be adopted, and shelters already run foster programs (the Hayden Law, 1998).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Rural zoning, contractor-grade building codes, land prices, and, since <i>Grants Pass</i> (2024), citations for sleeping outside.</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-1-2\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">1.2</span><div><h2>Collective-owned homes</h2><p>Owned together · All in</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Homes given to the program, held together and shared by match.</p><p>Dense or spread out. People who live at the commons have collective-owned homes too, just high-density ones. A single suburban house given in is a low-density one. Whoever lives there joins give-and-get: the backyard might hold a vegetable bed or the block’s water filter, meeting needs for the neighborhood and for them. Ready to move? Give your whole home, and get a comparable one. That’s how land moves from private control into shared hands: one home at a time, by choice.</p><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Two ways to give.</b> A <b>kin gift:</b> the home becomes collective-owned, and each home you give covers one household for good, your kids first or anyone you name, chosen family included. Or a <b>trade:</b> no sentimental tie, so you take your standing somewhere new and the home opens to the neighborhood. Either way, you can keep living in your home for life, and it goes to the program after.</li><li><b>Standing moves with you.</b> Going all in earns standing: a lasting promise of a home anywhere in California. Moving frees your home for someone else, so moving is welcome.</li><li><b>Inherit a house you don’t want?</b> Trade it. Once homes aren’t tied to jobs, Humboldt’s forests are open too.</li><li><b>Rank homes honestly.</b> The match is built so honesty is your best move, and nobody ends up with a home they like less than the one they gave. Homes people want most simply get more requests (top trading cycles, the idea behind kidney exchange).</li><li><b>Children first.</b> Families with kids get first pick of private homes, and the match keeps kids in their school district (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-10\">2.10 Least able first</a>).</li><li><b>Matched like college housing:</b> for how long, who must live with you, and whether you’re open to housemates.</li></ul><h4>When a home opens</h4><p>Everyone gets a home eventually. Until there are enough, this is who is fully covered first.</p><ol><li><b>Kin first rights:</b> one household for each home given, the giver’s kids first or whoever they name.</li><li><b>Someone already living there,</b> like the last resident’s grown kid.</li><li><b>Neighbors’ nominees,</b> closest neighbors first. Nominees meet the same need standard as everyone else.</li><li><b>Past residents</b> who marked themselves “open to offers.” Anyone marked “happy where I am” is never contacted.</li><li><b>The waitlist,</b> least able first (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-10\">2.10</a>).</li></ol><p><b>Nearest first, need decides.</b> The same rule runs for homes, shops and spaces (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-3\">2.3</a>).</p><h4>Homes owned by companies</h4><ul><li>Companies are people underneath, often pension funds for teachers and firefighters. Their needs are steady income and a clean way out.</li><li><b>Sell to the program</b> at an appraised price paid over years: steady income, with the tax spread out too.</li><li><b>Renters get first chance</b> to buy their homes as co-ops, and property managers can stay on as paid stewards.</li><li><b>Sell for less, and the difference counts as a gift.</b></li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> The ROAD to Housing Act (2026): from January 7, 2027, for-profit owners of 350 or more single-family homes can’t buy more, with exceptions like build-to-rent. It doesn’t force sales, so a way out matters.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> McKinney-Vento: students without stable housing can stay in their school of origin, with transportation.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Federal</span> Selling over time spreads the tax across years (installment sales, §453). Giving your home while keeping the right to live in it for life can count as a charitable gift (§170).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> A home trade is a change in ownership, which resets property tax to today’s value.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> Zoning for given homes, portable standing, and a statewide match. Doors for advocacy (<a href=\"#lens-governance/3.6\">3.6 Stairs for change</a>).</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-1-3\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">1.3</span><div><h2>Give-and-get program</h2><p>Private · Step in</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Your home stays yours. Opt in, and your land gets lighter rules for meeting needs.</p><p>Your land is your slice of heaven for as long as you live there and look after it, and it can pass to whoever you choose. Opting in rezones it into the program. It’s the step-in model, like joining a grocery co-op: get involved and benefit without going all in. <b>It’s invite-only:</b> you decide who stays, like a close friend in a tough spot. Open it to people you don’t know, after a meeting where either of you can say no, and you get the most support of all. Give in <b>ongoing gifts</b> or <b>one-time gifts</b>; every gift is a pledge, not a contract.</p><h4>Ways to give</h4><ul><li><b>Grow.</b> Gardeners grow food with you. You keep enough for your household, and the abundance goes to the commons. Start with one bed, and ask for watering help anytime.</li><li><b>Water and make.</b> Room for rain tanks, a filter station or a maker’s bench, with first use for you.</li><li><b>Care.</b> Dog walks, babysitting swaps, help for someone with a disability, meals after a birth or a loss.</li><li><b>Shelter.</b> Someone you invite sleeps in their car, a tent or a cabin. They owe you nothing. Host someone you don’t know, and you get a support worker, a mediator and the home-care fund behind you.</li><li><b>Bridge homes.</b> “My kids want it in five years.” Someone lives there until then, knowing it ends, with a commons home guaranteed next.</li><li><b>Foster.</b> Take in a shelter animal for a while, with food, vet care and backup care from the commons.</li><li><b>Lend it to friends.</b> Away every Christmas? Let a friend’s family stay. Nobody tracks it; a friend’s home instead of a hotel is already the gift.</li><li><b>Never-empty pledge.</b> Your home won’t sit unused for more than a year. Nobody takes it from you.</li></ul><h4>Shelter without a $500k ADU</h4><ul><li>A guest needs a safe place to sleep and a bathroom. A car, tent or small cabin works with <b>bathroom access</b> (yours, a commons station nearby, or a serviced portable or certified composting toilet), <b>smoke and CO alarms</b>, a fire extinguisher and a clear way out.</li></ul><h4>Safety for givers</h4><ul><li><b>A state-backed home-care fund</b> repairs damage. Damage means someone is struggling, so it’s met with cleaning help and care, not fines.</li><li><b>One check-in a year</b> on the house’s needs, by a neutral town steward or by you. Neighbors can flag the land, not the people.</li><li><b>You choose how much the home can change:</b> keep it as is, decorate and restore, or make it yours. Gardens, ramps and rain tanks stay.</li></ul><h4>Thanks, not rewards</h4><ul><li><b>Basics are never earned,</b> and everyday care isn’t counted. What gets written down is coordination, not credit (Alfie Kohn).</li><li><b>Safety without hoarding.</b> When health care, food and a home are reliably shared, hoarding has less and less to do.</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Prop 13 (1978) caps property tax at 1% of the last sale price, so the longer someone holds, the less they pay, even for an empty home.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Building codes treat anywhere people sleep as a home, and most places require a flush toilet. The shelter-crisis shortcut covers only public land.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> The Ellis Act stops cities from forcing owners to keep renting (and in August 2026 an appeals court tentatively said it preempts San Francisco’s empty-homes tax), so pledges stay voluntary.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Anything a guest gives for a stay can look like rent, making them a tenant with rules a car or tent can’t meet.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Owner move-in is a just cause to end a tenancy (SB 567): family moves in within 90 days and stays a year. That fits bridge homes.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Owners who let people garden are shielded from most injury suits (Civil Code §846), unless they get something in return. Food donors are liable only for gross negligence (AB 1219).</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> Some counties already pay landlords for damage when they house people leaving homelessness (Marin: up to $3,500). In-Home Supportive Services pays caregivers, family included.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A give-and-get zone with emergency-housing safety rules (like Appendix X of the state residential code) on private land, a hosted-shelter category and a home-care fund in every town.</li></ul></section><section class=\"gv-sec\" id=\"gv-1-4\"><div class=\"gv-sec-head\"><span class=\"gv-n\">1.4</span><div><h2>If you rent</h2><p>Renting</p></div></div><p class=\"gv-vshort\">Renters go first at the commons, with more ways to stay and to own.</p><p>Most people who rent don’t have land to give, so they get first access to what the commons offers. Landlords are welcome too; renters just go first. And renting can be a stable, good life, with real ways to stay put or own your home together.</p><h4>How needs get met</h4><ul><li><b>Renters first at the commons.</b> When garden beds, commons homes or spots in the match are limited, people without land go first.</li><li><b>Grow where you rent.</b> Pots on a balcony, patio or stoop are yours to plant, and gardeners can help (<a href=\"#lens-governance/1-3\">1.3 Grow</a>).</li><li><b>A lawyer if you face eviction,</b> free, everywhere in California, not only in San Francisco.</li><li><b>First chance to buy.</b> When your building sells, renters, with a land trust behind them, get the first offer and can turn it into a co-op (1.1).</li><li><b>Renters first for needs-meeting goods and jobs.</b> When goods or openings at needs-meeting businesses are limited, renters go before landlords (<a href=\"#lens-governance/2-1\">2.1</a>).</li><li><b>The same door for landlords.</b> A landlord who gives their extra homes to become collective-owned gets the same access. Everyone gets the same chance; where you stand with land only changes your first step, and nobody has to leave real safety to take it. It’s moving from betting on the market to betting on something fairer.</li><li><b>Join give-and-get with your landlord’s OK:</b> a shared garden bed, a rain tank, a babysitting swap.</li><li><b>Landlords have needs too:</b> steady income and a clean way out. Selling to the program over years meets both (1.2).</li></ul><h4>California today</h4><ul class=\"gv-law\"><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Tenant Protection Act (AB 1482, 2019) caps most rent increases at 5% plus inflation, never more than 10% a year, and requires a just cause to end most tenancies after a year.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> San Francisco’s Prop F (2018) gives every tenant facing eviction a free lawyer. 55.6% of voters said yes.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> The Neighborhood Food Act (AB 2561, 2014) lets renters grow food in portable containers.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Helps</span> SB 1079 (2020) and San Francisco’s COPA (2019) give tenants, land trusts and co-ops a chance to buy when certain homes sell.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">In the way</span> Costa-Hawkins (1995) blocks rent control on single-family homes and on newer buildings. Votes to repeal it failed in 2018, 2020 and 2024.</li><li><span class=\"gv-tag\">Not yet law</span> A statewide right to a lawyer in eviction, a statewide first chance to buy, and renters-first access to commons land.</li></ul></section><p class=\"gv-src\">Sources: <i>City of Santa Barbara v. Adamson</i>, 27 Cal.3d 123 (1980); Cal. Rev. &amp; Tax. Code §402.1; Williamson Act (1965); Rainwater Capture Act of 2012; Cal. Code Regs. tit. 25 (Class K); Cal. Gov. Code §8698.4; Cal. Const. art. XIII A (Prop 13); SB 1079 (2020); S.F. Admin. Code ch. 41B (COPA); AB 816, California Worker Cooperative Act (2015); Cal. Gov. Code §7060 (Ellis Act); SB 567 (2023); Cal. Civ. Code §846; AB 1219 (2017); Marin County Landlord Partnership Program; 21st Century ROAD to Housing Act (2026), Title X; McKinney-Vento Homeless Assistance Act, 42 U.S.C. §11431 et seq.; 26 U.S.C. §§170(f)(3), 453; Gale and Shapley (1962); Shapley and Scarf (1974); AB 1482, Tenant Protection Act (2019); S.F. Proposition F (2018); AB 2561, Neighborhood Food Act (2014); Costa-Hawkins Rental Housing Act (1995); Alfie Kohn, <i>Punished by Rewards</i> (1993); Elinor Ostrom, <i>Governing the Commons</i> (1990).</p>";

  /* Unit 2 · How it works: the big-picture system behind unit 1's land. Sections use dashed ids (gv-2-1 … gv-2-11) like unit 1. */
  function law(rows) { return '<h4>California today</h4><ul class="gv-law">' + rows.map(function (r) { return '<li><span class="gv-tag">' + r[0] + '</span> ' + r[1] + '</li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>'; }
  function sec2(n, title, kicker, short, body) {
    return '<section class="gv-sec" id="gv-' + n.replace('.', '-') + '"><div class="gv-sec-head"><span class="gv-n">' + n + '</span><div><h2>' + title + '</h2><p>' + kicker + '</p></div></div><p class="gv-vshort">' + short + '</p>' + body + '</section>';
  }
  function go(n, label) { return '<a href="#lens-governance/' + n.replace('.', '-') + '">' + (label || n) + '</a>'; }
  var U2SEC = [['2.1', 'Needs-meeting businesses'], ['2.2', 'Work that needs doing'], ['2.3', 'Shops and spaces'], ['2.4', 'Public jobs, kept'], ['2.5', 'Community safety'], ['2.6', 'Who decides'], ['2.7', 'Health together'], ['2.8', 'Safer together'], ['2.9', 'Taxes, time and gifts'], ['2.10', 'Least able first'], ['2.11', 'Sanctuary for animals']];
  var UNIT2 =
    '<div class="gv-unit" id="gv-u2"><span class="eyebrow">Unit 2</span><h2>How it works</h2><p>The big picture: the work that keeps needs met, who does it, who decides, how it’s paid for, and what keeps everyone safe.</p></div>' +
    '<section class="gv-sec"><p>Unit 1 started from where you live. This is the system those places share. It’s opt-in, and it grows a little at a time.</p>' +
    '<p><b>The good parts of taxes and law stay:</b> 911, the fire department, schools, roads, clean water and medical care. What changes is who gets support first, who decides, and how much can be given in time instead of money.</p>' +
    '<p>When needs are reliably met, there’s less to steal, fight or police over. If a 24/7 diner a few blocks away will always feed you, nobody needs to break in for food.</p>' +
    '<ol class="gv-u2list">' + U2SEC.map(function (s) { return '<li><a href="#lens-governance/' + s[0].replace('.', '-') + '"><span class="gv-n">' + s[0] + '</span>' + s[1] + '</a></li>'; }).join('') + '</ol></section>' +

    sec2('2.1', 'Needs-meeting businesses', 'All in or step in', 'Any business that meets real needs can go all in, or step in first with a fair-rate contract.',
      '<p>A needs-meeting business (NMB) sells what it makes, like any shop. What’s different is where the money goes. It can be a construction crew, a dental office, or a few people knitting socks together in someone’s living room while their coworkers work from home.</p>' +
      '<h4>Three musts to go all in</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Fair shares.</b> Profits are shared by time and effort, about the same per hour for every role. Building a home is a team effort: the designer, the contractor and the person framing walls bring apples-and-oranges abilities, and each hour counts about the same. No profit leaves to outside owners or executives.',
        '<b>Elected leaders.</b> Workers choose the people who coordinate them, and can choose again.',
        '<b>Public pay.</b> The town posts every NMB’s pay for anyone to see.']) +
      '<h4>Public servants, paid in money</h4><p>The community gives NMBs homes, spaces and gardens, so they’re public servants in spirit: paid in money, cared for by the people, working for the people. If prices look high and someone earns more than <b>twice the town’s median hourly pay</b>, a restorative practitioner invites them to talk it through with the community at a town meeting. It’s a conversation, not a fine.</p>' +
      '<h4>Enough, plus fun</h4><p>Shares are planned so everyone’s costs are covered, with money left over for fun. Living is more than getting by. The community credit union helps each business plan for that, unless housing and food are truly free, which is the hope.</p>' +
      '<h4>Ways in</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Invited first.</b> The commons reaches out to businesses already doing this work: “We’d love to back you, if you go all in.”',
        '<b>Step in with a fair-rate contract.</b> Not ready to go all in? Meet a need at a rate the town publishes each year.',
        '<b>Started by the town.</b> When no business meets a need, the town hires people to meet it directly. That work grows into its own NMB, guided by an <b>NMB business advisor</b>, a new public role.']) +
      '<h4>What NMBs get</h4>' + ul([
        '<b>Priority for homes</b> for people who keep meeting community needs, part-time included, and parents taking time off to raise kids. Nobody loses a home for taking someone in, or because someone in the household works a private job.',
        '<b>A shared health program</b> on top of regular insurance (' + go('2.7') + ').',
        '<b>Work from home</b> whenever the work allows.',
        '<b>Restorative justice from outside.</b> When conflict happens, trained practitioners from outside the business help everyone repair it (<a href="#lens-relationships/dialogue">Dialogue</a>).',
        '<b>Gardeners and garden educators</b> for the land they use, and <b>first pick of commons spaces</b> (' + go('2.3') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'The Worker Cooperative Act (AB 816, 2015): a worker co-op can share its surplus by hours worked or by pay (Corp. Code §12243), worker-members elect the board, and outside “community investors” can’t run it.'],
        ['Federal', 'Co-op surplus paid out to members (patronage dividends) is taxed once, to the member, not twice like corporate profits (Subchapter T, 26 U.S.C. §§1381–1388).'],
        ['Helps', 'Since 2023, California job postings must show a pay range (SB 1162, 2022). Public employees’ pay is already public.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'New York City has funded worker co-op development since 2014, a model for NMB business advisors.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'NMB status, posted pay for every member business, fair-rate contracts and NMB business advisors.']])) +

    sec2('2.2', 'Work that needs doing', 'Needs-meeting jobs', 'Start from rights and work backward to the jobs that make them real.',
      '<p>Water and a decent home are rights on paper (<a href="#lens-governance/3.1">3.1</a>). Making them real takes people. All of these can be NMBs, and get the most support when they are.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Water and power:</b> plumbers, well and rain-tank installers, water testers, solar installers. <b>Public utilities</b> (water, power, gas, sewer, garbage, internet, transit) support everyone. When a private company runs one, the town treats it as an outside business it pays, free to go all in.',
        '<b>Building and infrastructure:</b> builders, roofers, electricians, natural builders, and road, bridge and transit crews.',
        '<b>Homes:</b> home cleaners, home restorers, furniture and bed makers, movers.',
        '<b>Land:</b> land restoration crews, soil and pollution experts and testers, toxicologists (<a href="#lens-toxins">Poisons</a>), tree planters.',
        '<b>Food:</b> growers, garden educators, seed savers, compost and worm farms, food preservers (canning, drying, fermenting, shared freezers), bakers, cooks, grocers, and public kitchens, including a 24/7 diner within walking distance.',
        '<b>Health:</b> doctors, dentists who offer yearly exams, nutritionists, community health workers, herbalists, midwives, therapists.',
        '<b>Care:</b> childcare, elder care, disability care, pet care, veterinarians, animal sanctuaries and fosters.',
        '<b>Things:</b> seamstresses, cobblers, clothing and electronics repair, thrift, a tool library that sells cheap hardware, auto mechanics who offer yearly checkups, bike shops.',
        '<b>Place:</b> litter crews, artists and muralists, because a beautiful block is a need too.',
        '<b>Learning, play and discovery:</b> skill educators, coaches, musicians, performers, and scientists, with research funded and results open to all.',
        '<b>Holding it together:</b> community credit unions (with a student debt fund), mediators and restorative practitioners, home stewards, housing matchmakers, business matchmakers and NMB business advisors.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Public and co-op utilities already work: Sacramento’s public power (SMUD), rural electric co-ops, and community choice energy like CleanPowerSF.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A needs-meeting jobs list tied to rights, with commons support for the businesses doing them.']])) +

    sec2('2.3', 'Shops and spaces', 'Nearest first', 'Empty storefronts come back when space goes to whoever shows up with care.',
      ul([
        '<b>A public registry of unused spaces:</b> every empty storefront, kitchen and workshop, open for anyone to see.',
        '<b>Nearest first.</b> The people who live closest get the first claim on a space, then the rest of the neighborhood. Need breaks ties. The same rule runs for homes (' + go('1.2') + ').',
        '<b>Say what you want to open.</b> “I’m a baker and my friend’s a chef, and we’d like a restaurant down the block.” Nearby businesses hear about it and are asked if they’d share space or join in.',
        '<b>Pitch to who’s already there.</b> When someone else wants a restaurant, they pitch it to the existing one, which may welcome the help and the vision.',
        '<b>A neighborhood business council.</b> Anyone can come to the meetings. Residents who attend vote, and residents who can’t are asked by email or text.',
        '<b>Fewer rules to start.</b> With the commons always there as a safety net, working for yourself needs less protection, so it can need less paperwork. People work because they want money for fun, gifts or taxes, not out of fear.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'San Francisco taxes ground-floor storefronts left empty more than half the year (Prop D, 2020).'],
        ['Helps', 'Local boards must post agendas and take public comment (the Brown Act, 1953), a base for open neighborhood councils.'],
        ['County by county', 'Home businesses and new storefronts need local permits, and the rules vary.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A public space registry, nearest-first claims and neighborhood business councils.']])) +

    sec2('2.4', 'Public jobs, kept', 'The city in transition', 'Nobody loses their public job. The work changes shape, and everyone gains the commons.',
      '<p><b>The promise to public workers:</b> no layoffs. You keep your job, your pay, your pension and your union, and you gain full access to the commons. Public-sector unions are friends.</p>' +
      '<h4>Work that grows</h4><p>Fire, 911, schools, parks, libraries, public health, public works and water.</p>' +
      '<h4>Work that changes shape</h4><ul class="gv-shift">' + [
        ['Policing survival crimes, jails, eviction court', 'Safety officers, crisis responders, restorative practitioners'],
        ['Code enforcement and permit desks', 'Home stewards and NMB business advisors'],
        ['Means-testing and eligibility checks', 'Housing matchmakers and commons coordinators'],
        ['Shelter administration', 'Commons staff'],
        ['Parking and citation enforcement', 'Litter crews and street care']].map(function (r) { return '<li><span>' + r[0] + '</span><span aria-hidden="true">→</span><span class="sr-only">becomes</span><b>' + r[1] + '</b></li>'; }).join('') + '</ul>' +
      '<p><b>The honest worry:</b> commons land pays little or no property tax, so the city’s tax base shrinks. Its costs shrink too: shelters, jails, emergency room visits, eviction court. Pilots measure both, out loud.</p>' +
      law([['Not yet law', 'A no-layoffs transition plan that moves public workers into needs-meeting roles.']])) +

    sec2('2.5', 'Community safety', 'Help when you need it', 'Safety officers on call when people want them, and quiet ways to ask for help when someone can’t.',
      ul([
        '<b>911 stays,</b> and so does the fire department.',
        '<b>Town safety officers</b> are around when people need them: to keep someone safe, walk someone home, or calm a situation down.',
        '<b>Crisis responders</b> (medics and counselors) answer mental health calls.',
        '<b>Quiet ways to ask.</b> Some people can’t go to anyone openly, like someone being controlled at home or forced to work. Advocates check in gently, and a quiet signal at a diner, clinic or pharmacy can start help without anyone else knowing.',
        '<b>Repair after harm.</b> Restorative practitioners help everyone involved ask what happened, who was hurt, and what would make it right.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'San Francisco’s Street Crisis Response Team (2020) sends medics and counselors to mental health calls instead of police.'],
        ['Helps', 'The CRISES Act (AB 2054, 2020) funds community-based emergency response pilots.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Town safety officers in every commons, and a quiet-signal network for people in coercion.']])) +

    sec2('2.6', 'Who decides', 'Lovernance', 'No one person at the top. Many people decide, close to home.',
      '<p>A whole city run by one mayor and a small board is a bottleneck, and big campaigns reward whoever can afford the most marketing. Lovernance spreads decisions out.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Who votes:</b> people who live or work in a commons vote on it. Give-and-get neighbors vote on anything that touches their land.',
        '<b>Pitch a policy for your neighborhood,</b> and your neighbors vote on it.',
        '<b>A project board.</b> Every local project shows three things side by side: how much it matters to people, how big a lift it is, and what it costs in taxes or time. Residents rank them, and the top ones get built.',
        '<b>Topic councils</b> for water, housing, food and the rest. Specialists can run without big campaigns: short written statements, public forums, low spending limits.',
        '<b>Lottery panels.</b> A random, representative group of interested residents studies a question, hears from experts and decides or recommends, the way a jury does.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'Charter cities like San Francisco can change how they govern by a vote of residents (a charter amendment).'],
        ['Helps', 'Vallejo (2012) ran the first citywide participatory budget in the US: residents proposed and voted on how to spend part of the city budget.'],
        ['Elsewhere', 'East Belgium has had a permanent citizens’ council chosen by lottery since 2019.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Neighborhood policy votes, topic councils and lottery panels with real decision power.']])) +

    sec2('2.7', 'Health together', 'Prevention first', 'A shared health program for staying well, with hospitals for the big things.',
      '<p>People who eat more produce, walk more and feel less stressed tend to need less medical care, so health care can become a smaller, gentler industry.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>Staying well:</b> yearly checkups and dental exams, nutrition, movement, mental health, and care at home, close to where people live.',
        '<b>Care without insurance in the middle.</b> Doctors, dentists and veterinarians can step off insurance, charge fair rates they choose, and be taken care of by the commons with a home, a space and the shared program.',
        '<b>Hospitals for the big things.</b> A serious injury or cancer still goes to a hospital, through regular insurance or Medi-Cal. The program sits on top, never instead.']) +
      law([
        ['In the way', 'Small employers can’t band together to buy one big group health plan through an association (SB 1375, 2018), with narrow exceptions.'],
        ['In the way', 'A clinic membership (direct primary care) is a gray area: if a monthly fee covers broad care, the state may treat it as a health plan that needs a license (Knox-Keene Act, 1975).'],
        ['Helps', 'Community health centers already charge on a sliding scale, and Medi-Cal has paid community health workers for prevention since 2022.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A shared health program licensed as its own kind of plan.']])) +

    sec2('2.8', 'Safer together', 'Insurance and disasters', 'Safety for every kind of land: commons, collective-owned homes and give-and-get.',
      ul([
        '<b>Disaster means a home tonight.</b> If a home burns or floods, the commons houses you right away while co-op builders rebuild. That promise is the real safety, more than any check.',
        '<b>Insurance owned by the people it covers.</b> A members’ mutual for all three kinds of land: no shareholders, and any surplus goes back into fire-hardening, not profit.',
        '<b>Harden the whole block together.</b> Neighbors, gardeners and time gifts do the work (ember-resistant vents, a clear 5-foot zone, irrigated gardens as green buffers), which earns home and community discounts.',
        '<b>Automatic payouts.</b> A neighborhood policy pays the local fund as soon as a fire or flood is measured, with no claims or adjusters.',
        '<b>A way out of the riskiest places.</b> Standing moves with you, so people can move somewhere safer without losing what they gave (' + go('1.2') + ').']) +
      law([
        ['In the way', 'Private insurers have pulled back, and the state’s last-resort FAIR Plan grew to about 697,000 policies by June 2026, up 157% since 2022. The Sustainable Insurance Strategy lets insurers raise rates if they write more in high-risk areas; whether that’s happening is disputed.'],
        ['Helps', '“Safer from Wildfires” (2022) requires insurers to give discounts for home and community fire-hardening, such as Firewise communities.'],
        ['Helps', 'Fremont (2024) became the first U.S. city to buy its own parametric flood policy, which pays automatically once flooding is measured. The California Earthquake Authority (1996) shows a publicly managed statewide pool can work.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A members’ mutual for lovernance land, and a statewide wildfire pool like the Earthquake Authority.']])) +

    sec2('2.9', 'Taxes, time and gifts', 'Paying for land', 'Money still matters, mostly for land. Time counts, and so do gifts of money.',
      '<p>The biggest cost of holding land is property tax. Lovernance keeps what taxes do well, lets more of it be given in time, and makes giving money easy for people who have more money than time.</p>' +
      ul([
        '<b>The commons owes no property tax,</b> so living there costs nothing in taxes. Without a job, you owe nothing, and being out of work stops being a crisis.',
        '<b>Local taxes in time.</b> Hours at a school, on a road crew or in a commons garden count toward property tax.',
        '<b>Money as a gift.</b> Someone with savings and little time can give money instead, to a neighbor or to the commons fund. A direct gift can go to one person, like a single mom out of work. A gift to the commons fund can’t be earmarked, but it’s tax-deductible.',
        '<b>Give and get.</b> “We’ll bring you food and elder care if you help cover your neighbor’s property tax this year.” People trade what they have more of, time or money.',
        '<b>A student debt fund</b> at the community credit union, which also helps the commons and NMBs plan their money together.',
        '<b>Estates and wills.</b> Give your home and keep living there for life, with a tax deduction now. Each home you give covers one household for good, your kids first (' + go('1.2') + '), so your family always has a home.',
        '<b>Everyday help isn’t counted.</b> Only bigger pledges get written down, for coordination (<a href="#lens-governance/1-3">1.3 Thanks, not rewards</a>).']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California exempts low-income housing owned by nonprofits from property tax (the welfare exemption, R&amp;T Code §214), so commons homes can be fully exempt today.'],
        ['Federal', 'There’s no federal property tax, and someone with no income owes no federal income tax.'],
        ['Federal', 'Anyone can give up to $19,000 per person a year (2026) with no gift tax return; above that it’s a form, with no tax due until $15 million in lifetime gifts. Gifts to a nonprofit fund are deductible.'],
        ['Federal', 'Giving your home while keeping the right to live in it for life can count as a charitable gift (26 U.S.C. §170(f)(3)).'],
        ['In the way', 'Prop 19 (2020): children keep their parents’ low property tax only if they move into the home. Keeping “the family house” is getting expensive; a kin gift to the commons offers another way.'],
        ['On the ballot', 'Prop 40 (November 3, 2026): a one-time 5% tax on billionaires’ wealth for health care, food aid and schools. Lovernance gifts are the voluntary version.'],
        ['Not here yet', 'California has no property tax work-off. Massachusetts lets towns take a set amount off an older homeowner’s tax for volunteer work (Gen. Laws ch. 59 §5K); in Wilbraham it’s up to $1,000 a year.'],
        ['Federal', 'Trading goods or services as a deal can count as taxable income (IRS barter rules). Gifts and favors given freely, with no deal, are different.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Paying local taxes in time, a pool for neighbors’ taxes, and a student debt fund at every community credit union.']])) +

    sec2('2.10', 'Least able first', 'Who goes first', 'When there isn’t enough yet, people with the fewest ways to meet their own needs go first.',
      ul([
        '<b>The first pilot:</b> the lowest-income schoolchildren and their families, arranged by school district, get permanent homes in the program.',
        '<b>Families with children</b> get first pick of private homes, and the match keeps kids in their school (' + go('1.2') + ').',
        '<b>Renters before landlords</b> for needs-meeting goods and jobs (' + go('1.4') + ').',
        '<b>Then outward:</b> disabled people, elders and anyone without a home, and from there, everyone.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'The Local Control Funding Formula (2013) gives schools extra money for each low-income student, English learner and foster youth, so districts already know where to start.'],
        ['Federal', 'McKinney-Vento: students without stable housing can stay in their school of origin, with transportation.'],
        ['Not yet law', 'A district-by-district pilot that houses the lowest-income students’ families for good.']])) +

    sec2('2.11', 'Sanctuary for animals', 'Nonviolent land', 'Lovernance land is nonviolent land. No animal is killed there.',
      ul([
        '<b>No killing on lovernance land.</b> When land joins, it promises nonviolence: no slaughter, no hunting and no poison bait. City animals can find sanctuary there too.',
        '<b>Not about your plate.</b> People eat what they choose, and nobody checks anyone’s lunch. Once backyard vegetables are easy and fun, fewer people feel they need meat.',
        '<b>Fostering is a give-and-get gift.</b> Shelter animals can live with anyone in the commons who wants them, with food, vet care and backup care (' + go('1.1') + ').',
        '<b>Sanctuaries for farmed animals</b> wherever the land fits: rescued hens, goats and sheep living out their lives.',
        '<b>Sheep instead of mowers.</b> Picture a quiet city-block backyard with the fences down, gone wild again and grazed by a small flock. It starts when neighbors agree, and as homes become collective-owned, the last fences come down in their own time.',
        '<b>Why it matters:</b> a slaughterhouse next to homes is loud, unsafe and hard to live beside. A sanctuary meets needs for animals and people at once.']) +
      law([
        ['Helps', 'California bans animal cruelty (Penal Code §597) and the smallest cages for some farm animals (Prop 12, 2018).'],
        ['County by county', 'San Francisco lets a household keep two goats without a permit; more, or other hoofed animals like sheep, take a permit (Health Code §27).'],
        ['Helps', 'A promise can run with the land: deed covenants bind later owners (Civil Code §1468), and conservation easements protect habitat for good (Civil Code §815).'],
        ['Not yet law', 'Nonviolent land as its own zoning category, with sanctuary rules for grazing animals in cities.']])) +

    '<p class="gv-src">Sources: AB 816, California Worker Cooperative Act (2015), Cal. Corp. Code §12243; 26 U.S.C. §§1381–1388; SB 1162 (2022); NYC Worker Cooperative Business Development Initiative (2014); SMUD; CleanPowerSF; S.F. Proposition D (2020); Ralph M. Brown Act (1953); S.F. Street Crisis Response Team (2020); AB 2054, CRISES Act (2020); S.F. Charter; Vallejo participatory budgeting (2012); Ostbelgien Citizens’ Council (2019); SB 1375 (2018); Knox-Keene Health Care Service Plan Act of 1975; Medi-Cal community health worker benefit (2022); Cal. Code Regs. tit. 10 (Safer from Wildfires, 2022); California FAIR Plan data (June 2026); City of Fremont parametric flood policy (2024); California Earthquake Authority; Cal. Rev. &amp; Tax. Code §214; IRS annual gift exclusion and lifetime exemption (2026); 26 U.S.C. §170(f)(3); Cal. Const. art. XIII A §2.1 (Prop 19); Proposition 40 (2026); Mass. Gen. Laws ch. 59 §5K; IRS Topic 420, bartering income; Local Control Funding Formula (2013); McKinney-Vento Homeless Assistance Act, 42 U.S.C. §11431 et seq.; Cal. Penal Code §597; Proposition 12 (2018); S.F. Health Code §27; Cal. Civ. Code §§815, 1468.</p>';

  function view(sub) {
    var cur = /^(u3|3)/.test(sub || '') ? 'u3' : /^(u2|2)/.test(sub || '') ? 'u2' : 'u1';
    var html =
      '<section class="gv-hero"><span class="gv-pill">Tier 1 · Choices · Beta</span><h1 tabindex="-1">Governance</h1>' +
      '<p class="gv-lede">Laws shape how people can meet their needs, and how to advocate within existing systems while we build better ones. <b>Every shared need (water, air, land, a place to sleep) is governed by someone,</b> often removed from the people their choices affect. It starts with a new vision of governance we can build, then gets into what the law currently is, including rights you hold just by existing.</p>' +
      '<p class="gv-note">This points to some federal laws, state laws, and county or city rules. This is not legal advice. For your own situation, free legal aid or the California Courts Self-Help Guide (selfhelp.courts.ca.gov) can help.</p>' +
        '<ul class="gv-units" aria-label="Units">' + [['u1', '1 · Lovernance'], ['u2', '2 · How it works'], ['u3', '3 · The law today']].map(function (u) {
          var on = u[0] === cur; return '<li' + (on ? ' class="now"' : '') + '><a href="#lens-governance/' + u[0] + '"' + (on ? ' aria-current="true"' : '') + '>' + u[1] + '</a></li>';
        }).join('') + '</ul></section>' +

      UNIT1 +
      UNIT2 +

      '<div class="gv-unit" id="gv-u3"><span class="eyebrow">Unit 3</span><h2>The law today</h2>' +
        '<p>The rights, rules and decision-makers we live under now, and how to move them.</p></div>' +

      sec('3.1', 'Rights you have by existing', 'Technically, what you’re entitled to just by being a person here.',
        '<p>Both constitutions mostly protect people from the government, rather than promising what people need. Neither names a right to food, water or a home.</p>' +
        ul(['<b>US Constitution:</b> speech, religion, assembly and petitioning the government (1st Amendment); no unreasonable searches (4th); due process and equal protection of the laws (5th and 14th); no cruel and unusual punishment (8th). Most of these protect every person in the US, not only citizens.',
          '<b>California Constitution, Article I:</b> “All people are by nature free and independent and have inalienable rights. Among these are enjoying and defending life and liberty, acquiring, possessing, and protecting property, and pursuing and obtaining safety, happiness, and privacy.” It also protects speech, assembly, and your right to see government meetings and records (§3).',
          '<b>Water:</b> Article X, §2 of the state constitution says water must be put to reasonable, beneficial use, not wasted.',
          '<b>A decent home:</b> the Housing Act of 1949 set a national goal of “a decent home and a suitable living environment for every American family” (42 U.S.C. §1441). It’s a stated goal rather than a right a court enforces, which makes it a strong promise to hold Congress to.']) +
        '<p class="gv-note"><b>Animals and land:</b> neither constitution gives rights to animals, rivers or land; in law they’re mostly property. California protects animals from cruelty (Penal Code §597) and some farm animals from the smallest cages (Prop 12, 2018). Ecuador’s constitution (2008) recognizes rights of nature.</p>' +
        '<p class="gv-src">Sources: US Constitution, Amendments 1, 4, 5, 8 and 14; California Constitution, art. I §§1–3 and art. X §2; Housing Act of 1949, 42 U.S.C. §1441; Cal. Penal Code §597; Proposition 12 (2018); Constitution of Ecuador (2008), arts. 71–74.</p>') +

      sec('3.2', 'Two views, the same needs', 'Strategies differ by place; the needs underneath are shared.',
        '<div class="gv-views">' +
        '<div class="gv-view"><span class="eyebrow">In a big city</span><p>“I’d prefer to buy tap water than lug it out of some river myself. I wish it were healthier and cheaper, but I’ll happily pay for the convenience. I’m open to collecting rainwater for my plants.”</p><small>Needs: ease, health, time, accessibility</small></div>' +
        '<div class="gv-view"><span class="eyebrow">In rural California</span><p>“Why does big agriculture get water rights to supposedly provide for the collective, in a way that doesn’t allow me to provide for myself in my home, to grow my own food? The irrational red tape is frustrating.”</p><small>Needs: fairness, autonomy, food, empowerment</small></div>' +
        '</div><p>Both are reasonable. Conflicts live between strategies, never between needs, so good governance starts by asking what everyone needs and who is at the table.</p>') +

      sec('3.3', 'Who controls California’s water', 'Each decision has a holder, a way they’re chosen, and a door to knock on.',
        '<div class="gv-tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Who holds it</th><th>How they’re chosen</th><th>How to reach them</th></tr></thead><tbody>' +
        WHO.map(function (r) { return '<tr>' + r.map(function (c) { return '<td>' + c + '</td>'; }).join('') + '</tr>'; }).join('') +
        '</tbody></table></div>' +
        '<p class="gv-note"><b>Worth knowing:</b> some agricultural water districts give votes by land owned rather than one person, one vote, a system the US Supreme Court upheld in 1973. The biggest landowners can hold the most votes.</p>') +

      sec('3.4', 'Where the water goes', 'In an average year, across the whole state.',
        '<div class="gv-split"><div style="--w:50%"><b>About 50%</b><span>Environment: rivers, wetlands, the Delta</span></div><div style="--w:40%"><b>About 40%</b><span>Farms</span></div><div style="--w:10%"><b>About 10%</b><span>Cities and homes</span></div></div>' +
        '<p>Of farm water, about 27% grows feed for animals: alfalfa, irrigated pasture and silage corn. Wet years and dry years shift these numbers a lot; in droughts, rivers lose the most.</p>' +
        '<p class="gv-src">Source: Public Policy Institute of California. Water per pound of each food is in <a href="#lens-water/sources/take">the end of Water: Sources</a>.</p>') +

      '<aside class="gv-fun"><span class="eyebrow">Fun fact</span><p>California law (2012) recognizes a human right to safe, clean, affordable water. Rights on paper become rights in practice when people keep asking.</p></aside>' +

      sec('3.5', 'Criminalization and incarceration', 'When meeting a need becomes a crime.',
        '<p>Sleeping, sitting, camping, parking overnight, washing up: these are ways people meet the needs for rest, shelter and cleanliness. In many cities they’re against the law in public, which falls hardest on people who have nowhere private to do them.</p>' +
        ul(['<b>Since <i>City of Grants Pass v. Johnson</i></b> (US Supreme Court, 2024), cities can enforce bans on sleeping and camping outside even when there are no shelter beds. That July, California’s Governor ordered state agencies to clear encampments and urged cities to do the same.',
          '<b>Fines grow.</b> A ticket someone can’t pay can become a warrant, a suspended license or jail time, and a record makes housing and work harder to find, which makes the next ticket more likely.',
          '<b>Incarceration at scale.</b> The US holds about 1.9 million people in prisons, jails and detention, one of the highest rates in the world. California’s state prisons hold about 90,000.',
          '<b>Voters have moved both ways.</b> Prop 47 (2014) reduced some drug and theft felonies to misdemeanors; Prop 36 (2024) raised penalties for some of them again. Prop 6 (2024), which would have ended forced labor as a punishment in the state constitution, did not pass.',
          '<b>Other strategies being tried:</b> diversion to treatment instead of jail, restorative justice circles, and crisis teams of medics and counselors instead of police for mental health calls (CAHOOTS in Eugene, Oregon, since 1989; San Francisco’s Street Crisis Response Team).']) +
        '<p class="gv-note"><b>Two questions for any rule like this:</b> What need was the person meeting? Does the rule offer another way to meet it?</p>' +
        '<p>More in <a href="#lens-relationships/special/distress">Relationships: People in distress</a> and <a href="#lens-relationships/special/power">Power &amp; peace</a>.</p>' +
        '<p class="gv-src">Sources: <i>City of Grants Pass v. Johnson</i>, 603 U.S. (2024); California Executive Order N-1-24 (2024); Prison Policy Initiative, <i>Mass Incarceration: The Whole Pie</i> (2024); California Department of Corrections and Rehabilitation population reports; California Secretary of State, statements of vote (2014, 2024).</p>') +

      sec('3.6', 'Stairs for change', 'Start on the first step. Each one makes the next easier.',
        '<ol class="gv-stairs">' + STAIRS.map(function (s, i) {
          return '<li style="--i:' + i + '"><b>' + s[0] + '</b><p>' + s[1] + '</p>' + (s[2] ? '<a href="' + s[2] + '">' + s[3] + ' →</a>' : '') + '</li>';
        }).join('') + '</ol>' +
        '<p class="gv-src">Find your state representatives at findyourrep.legislature.ca.gov.</p>') +

      sec('3.7', 'Paths outside law', 'Not every change waits on a vote.',
        '<p>Advocating for yourself can mean asking the people and places already around you to meet a need directly, and leaving law out of it.</p>' +
        ul(['<b>Your church, temple, mosque or synagogue:</b> a parking lot opened for safe overnight parking, showers or a kitchen shared, land offered for homes. Since 2024, California lets faith groups build affordable housing on land they own without a rezoning fight (SB 4).',
          '<b>Your building or block:</b> a tenants’ association, a shared tool shelf, a meal train.',
          '<b>Co-ops and land trusts:</b> homes and workplaces owned by the people who use them (<a href="#lens-relationships/special/coops">Cooperatives</a>).',
          '<b>Mutual aid:</b> neighbors meeting neighbors’ needs directly, no application required.']) +
        '<p class="gv-src">Sources: California SB 4, Affordable Housing on Faith and Higher Education Lands Act of 2023.</p>') +

      sec('3.8', 'A transparency checklist', 'Questions to ask about any decision over a shared need.',
        ul(['Who made this decision, and who could have?', 'Who benefits, and who pays?', 'Who was at the table, and who wasn’t?', 'Where is the data, and can anyone read it?', 'When is the next chance to weigh in?'])) +

      '<p class="gv-note"><b>Coming next · Housing:</b> who owns homes today, and how to change hands toward the people who live in them (private equity is in 1.2).</p>';
    return { title: 'Governance · Kinship', html: mn().header('home') + '<div><a class="back" href="#">← Education</a></div><main class="gv">' + html + '</main>' + mn().footer() };
  }

